Kernel breadth expansion (2026-10-13)
===================================

This adds 31 semantic probes in 13 source files, following the 26 passes in
COVERAGE-FOURTH. These are 13 subsystem reviews, not a claim that the kernel
is comprehensively traced. The original pass count measured operations, not
coverage of every subsystem or driver. See coverage-fifth.json for the exact
source hashes, argument types, selectors and profiles.

Execution context and early boot
-------------------------------

Never add a probe merely because an SDT macro compiles at that location.
In this source tree, sys/sys/sdt.h uses asm-goto patchpoints; on amd64 the
inactive instruction is the five-byte NOP defined in
sys/amd64/include/sdt_machdep.h. sys/cddl/dev/sdt/sdt.c patches sites only
when enabled. Before enablement, the argument expressions and probe call
are bypassed. Without KDTRACE_HOOKS the macros disappear. kern_sdt.c's
fallback stub is NOT an early-boot tracing facility; it must never be called.

Consequently, shared runtime routines such as device attach and AHCI reset
may contain inactive sites during boot, but their pre-enable invocations
produce no records. These additions do not provide a boot trace, replay old
events, or force DTrace initialization earlier. Do not interpret absence of
boot records as absence of boot work or a successful operation.

DTrace's dtrace_probe_enter() explicitly forbids recursion (except its ERROR
probe) and disables interrupts. Do not place probes in DTrace internals,
probe patching, trap/NMI entry, low-level interrupt dispatch, IPI/rendezvous,
pmap/bootstrap allocation, or debugger/panic machinery without a separate
architecture and call-chain review. No new sites here are in those paths.
Entropy harvesting was considered and deferred: early initialization and
low-level callers deserve a separate review, not blanket instrumentation.

Interrupt probes are in intr_event_execute_handlers(), called from the
ordinary ithread execution loop. They run only after dead, suspended,
filter-only, and unneeded software handlers have been skipped. The start
site is AFTER acquisition of Giant when required; done is BEFORE release.
This measures the callback's elapsed time, including preemption/blocking,
not Giant acquisition time, CPU-only time, hard IRQ arrival latency, filter
execution, or interrupt-to-thread scheduling delay. No instrumentation was
added to intr_event_handle(), fast filters, clock filters, NMI or IPI paths.
DTrace uses its own low-level timer/interrupt machinery; it must not be
routed through these callbacks from probe context.

VM probes use existing object lifetime and lock guarantees. They do not
allocate, acquire locks, traverse object lists, inspect page contents, or
retain references. Pager completion is before object destruction. The
collapse event reports only the surviving object after reacquiring its
lock; it never passes the already-freed backing object. The bypass event
runs while both objects remain protected and before backing unlock. No
hooks were added to vm_object_init(), UMA constructors, bootstrap objects,
pmap, or page-fault entry.

The previous vm_fault_trap probes are in the normal fault resolver, not in
machine trap entry. On the validated amd64 path, trap_check() invokes
DTrace's no-fault handler before trap()/trap_pfault()/vm_fault_trap(). That
ordering is important and must be preserved. This review does not certify
all architectures or make instrumenting trap_check() permissible.

Contracts (arguments are zero-based)
------------------------------------

All pointer arguments are opaque identities, valid only in their documented
context. Profiles never dereference them. Identities can be reused. Native
return values are not automatically errno values or end-to-end success.
Double underscores in source probe names appear as hyphens in DTrace.

  irq:::thread-start / thread-done
    (intr_event *, intr_handler *), at the threaded callback boundary above.
    Both hardware interrupt threads and requested software handlers count.
    A thread can execute several handlers for one interrupt; counts are NOT
    hardware IRQ counts. Handler pointers identify callbacks, not IRQ vectors.

  vm:::object-pager-start / object-pager-done
    start(object, object type); done(object). Only vm_pager_deallocate() in
    vm_object_terminate(), excluding earlier pip waits/page cleanup and later
    object destruction. The pager returns void; done is not an error status.
  vm:::object-collapse
    (surviving object), after backing termination and survivor lock reacquire.
  vm:::object-bypass
    (surviving object, bypassed backing object), after unlink/refcount drop,
    with backing still locked and referenced. Reports successful bypass only.
    Neither event accounts for failed attempts or every object allocation.

  newbus:::attach-start / attach-done; detach-start / detach-done
    start(device); done(device, native driver callback result). DEVICE_ATTACH
    and DEVICE_DETACH only. Excludes disabled-device/busy/precheck failures,
    notifications, cleanup, and the complete device lifecycle. Post-enable
    hotplug/reprobe/detach can be observed; early boot cannot be reconstructed.

  affinity:::set-start / set-done
    start(level, which, id); done(level, which, id, errno).
    Entire kern_cpuset_setaffinity(), including capability/empty-mask/invalid
    selector failures. Excludes user mask copying and does not capture masks.

  routing:::action-start / action-done
    start(fibnum, action); done(fibnum, action, errno). rib_action() only.
    Direct calls to newer route APIs that bypass rib_action are NOT covered.
    No route addresses, next-hop contents, or packet data are captured.

  ndp:::state
    (llentry identity, old state, requested new state), immediately before
    the assignment in nd6_llinfo_setstate(). Includes same-state assignments;
    not all direct state assignments elsewhere. Timer setup precedes this
    event. Uses ndp rather than a trailing-digit provider name to avoid
    libdtrace interpreting an unavailable provider as a pid-provider request.

  geom:::access-start / access-done
    start(provider, delta-read, delta-write, delta-exclusive);
    done(provider, native callback result). Only the class access callback,
    not earlier validation failures, count updates or complete g_access().

  bpf:::read-wait-start / read-wait-done
    start(capture descriptor); done(descriptor, msleep result).
    The buffered-read data wait only; excludes hold-buffer ownership waits,
    zero-copy mode, packet contents and transfer to userspace. Timeout results
    can subsequently lead to a successful read; this is not the read result.

  fusefs:::answer-wait-start / answer-wait-done
    start(dispatcher); done(dispatcher, fticket_wait_answer result).
    Raw wait after enqueue, before later daemon/response error translation.
    Zero does not assert that the FUSE request ultimately succeeded.

  ahci:::reset-start / reset-done
    start(device); done(device, 0 or ENXIO). Controller reset including BIOS
    handoff, reset polling and capability restoration, not channel recovery.
    Boot invocations before enablement are absent.

  vtnet:::reinit-start / reinit-done
    start(softc); done(softc, errno). vtnet_reinit() including feature and
    queue setup. Excludes stop and later interrupt activation by the caller.

  iflib:::suspend-start / suspend-done; resume-start / resume-done
    start(context); done(context, native callback result). IFDI_SUSPEND and
    IFDI_RESUME only, under the existing context lock. Resume includes rollback
    invocations. Excludes prepare, taskqueue draining, child-bus callbacks and
    later state changes. This is not whole-system suspend/resume tracing.

  ipsec:::output-start / output-done
    start(address family); done(address family, native result).
    ipsec4/6_process_packet submission wrappers, not every IPsec path. The
    packet/policy may be consumed asynchronously, so neither is passed after
    the call. Results including EINPROGRESS/EJUSTRETURN retain native meaning;
    zero does not mean crypto or wire delivery completed.

Installed base profiles
-----------------------

The bsdinstruments Makefile automatically selects these 13 new .d files for
/usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles in the bsdinstruments base package:

  interrupt-threads       vm-object-operations    device-callbacks
  affinity-changes        routing-actions        neighbor-states
  geom-access             capture-waits          fuse-answer-waits
  ahci-resets             virtio-network-reinit   iflib-power-callbacks
  ipsec-submissions

The profile catalog now has 323 entries in this working tree. This checks
installation selection; it does not mean packages or files were installed.
The earlier base library/tool probes and profiles remain described in
COVERAGE-FOURTH. No new utility binary or dependency is required here.

Profiles use per-thread nesting for synchronous pairs and only scalar
arguments. Elapsed histograms include blocking/preemption and probe overhead.
Start tracing before the workload. Event drops, enabling/disabling midway,
and nonlocal exits can invalidate pairing. PID filters select the process
executing the probe, not workload causality: IRQ, worker and daemon activity
usually belongs to kernel threads. Use interrupt-threads without a target
application PID when investigating interrupt load. Provider names are not
necessarily loaded on the running kernel; an empty profile is not proof of
coverage or absence of work.

Coverage audit and remaining work
---------------------------------

coverage-kernel-census.py records direct calls AND known wrappers (including
IP, TCP, UDP, UDPLITE, CAM, MIB_SDT and Linuxulator wrappers) in kernel source
and headers. The JSON evidence is a search aid, NOT a coverage percentage:
header macros expand at callers; conditional compilation changes availability;
FBT, syscall, lockstat, profile and audit have dynamic mechanisms; provider
presence says nothing about semantic completeness. IPv6 and CAM already had
wrapper-based tracing and must not be described as wholly uninstrumented.
CAM recovery already has a result probe; this round does not duplicate it.

Reviewed and extended: interrupt threads, VM objects, device lifecycle,
CPU affinity, routing control, IPv6 neighbor state, GEOM access, BPF waiting,
FUSE IPC waiting, AHCI reset, virtio networking reinit, iflib PM, IPsec output.

Still partial: VM allocation/reclamation/swap/NUMA, network protocols and
routing APIs outside these wrappers, VFS/file locking/quotas, CAM transports,
GEOM classes, scheduler/taskqueues/callouts, jail/IPC/resource accounting,
LinuxKPI and Linuxulator, MAC/audit, and most individual device drivers.
Existing probes in these areas are not a substitute for per-operation review.

Further driver-family reviews include NVMe queue/completion/recovery beyond
existing reset probes, virtio block/SCSI, USB and audio beyond drain paths,
input/HID, MMC/SD, I2C/SPI/GPIO, firmware/ACPI, hypervisors, wireless and
individual NIC families. Device-specific locks, callback lifetime, debugger
or netdump reuse, and interrupt context require inspection before placement.
Imported ZFS and external graphics/hardware ports need separate ownership and
build reviews. No changes here diagnose the recorded ZFS/WITNESS issue.

Deferred context-sensitive areas: early bootstrap, trap/NMI/IPI entry,
pmap/allocator primitives used by tracing, entropy collection, and DTrace
itself. Those are deliberately not treated as a checklist of places to add
probes. Ordinary DTrace cannot retroactively observe pre-enable execution.

Validation and reproduction
---------------------------

From /usr/src, with permission to write /usr/obj:

  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-fifth-compile.py \
    --kernel-obj /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-fifth-check.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-fifth-objects.py
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/coverage-kernel-census.py

Compilation uses all logical CPUs and the existing configured amd64 kernel
and module object trees. Hooks enabled/disabled are both checked with -Werror.
Object inspection checks each declared probe's symbol, executable tracepoint
relocation and inactive amd64 NOP, plus absence in the no-hooks object.
Prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG and affected modules are also linked separately.

Results and before-images are under
/usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-kernel-breadth-validation. The running host has no
/dev/dtrace. Offline -e -Z profile checks permit absent providers, so they
are supplemented by selector/argument contracts and compiled object checks.
They cannot prove live provider registration, firing, pair balance, interrupt
latency impact, or hardware safety. A booted test kernel with DTrace is still
required for live workload and enable/disable stress qualification. No kernel
or module was loaded, installed or published, and no reboot was performed.
